														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4862538">
									

<p>Hello SuperAmazingTaco! let's take a look :) welcome to the site!</p>
<p>SAND</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: SCP-XXXX is a Rubik's cube which, when close to being solved, begins to behave like a 4-dimensional Rubik's cube to prevent itself from being solved. When it nears being solved again it will instead begin to behave like a 5-dimensional Rubik's cube, and so on. After being far away enough from being solved it will revert to a lower dimension. These processes have a consistent order and pattern. As a byproduct SCP-XXXXs mass increases with each dimension over 3 dimensions it contains (starting with the mass of a regular Rubik's cube) as well as giving off a small amount of ionizing radiation. This increase in mass and the presence of radiation grows exponentially with each additional dimension.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My main issue with the concept, is the skips overall purpose. what is the skip meant to do? whats the reason for it existing?</p>
<p>My second biggest issue comes from the effects it has; Currently, they don't really feel related to the skip. There also isn't much context as to why radiation and mass adds onto the skip after each near solve.- Currently these two aspects aren't really explained or expanded upon in the narrative, which causes the skip to feel a bit disjointed.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The central narrative of this piece would take the form of several testing logs where Dr. Cass experiments with SCP-XXXX and its unusual properties. Over time they work towards the possibility of using SCP-XXXXs radioactive properties for fuel in a power generator based on fusion reactor designs. This pursuit eventually leads to conflict with the site director who is against the idea. By the end of it the site director removes Dr. Cliff as the project lead (instead having them as a project advisor) and further experimentation with SCP-XXXX is delayed until a new project lead can be established.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How would you plan to execute this idea in a draft? currently the narrative doesn't show much of a progression.- how do tensions rise between the researcher and site director? how do ideas suddenly shift to nuclear reactor? what ends up causing the researcher to be removed from their position?</p>
<p>While I'm liking the conflict here, the site director doesn't seem to have a reason to be so opposed to this. Considering there's no immediate dangers (at least from whats described). It's odd that he/she would be ready to remove the researcher from their position in order to prevent further discussion on the topic. As a result, the conflict feels a bit forced/in-genuine as the opposition just sounds like their trying to make problems.</p>
<p>- Furthermore, it makes it harder for me to get invested in the characters (and/or get emotionally invested in the narrative). As the characters don't have any clear or conflicting goals.</p>
<p>Along with this, the narrative doesn't give much of a setup; as it stands, there isn't much on how the Foundation initially found the skip, or what caught their attention (the Rubik's cube on its own is rather a subtle anomaly, so it very likely the foundation didn't immediately suspect it).</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques/perspectives in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

